g jump. To get max jump, Mario needs running speed. When stuck against the wall, speed is 0, so jump height is lower.\n\nSolution: back off, then run and jump. Or better: since the pipe is an obstacle, use the approach: hold right to build speed, then jump earlier (before hitting the wall). My cycle had 12 frames of right+b (no jump) then A. But he's already at the wall, so those 12 frames don't build speed.\n\nBetter: move left a bit, then run right and jump. But the cycle repeats; once at the wall he never backs up.\n\nAlternative: use a longer jump hold and approach from further back. Let's load_state slot 1 (level start, x=40, before the pipe) — actually the checkpoint is at level start. Then replay with a better cycle: run right for a while, then jump when near the pipe. But timing is tricky.\n\nActually, more robust: in SMB, to clear a tall pipe you jump while running. From the level start, the pipe is at x~800-900. A well-timed full jump clears it.\n\nSimplest robust approach: hold right+B and press A every ~20 frames but hold A for the full 20 (so he's almost always jumping). Actually he'd be jumping continuously; when he jumps near the pipe with speed, he clears.\n\nThe issue is he got stuck because the jump cycle pressed A while at the wall with no speed.\n\nLet me try: from current stuck state, hold Left for a few frames to back off ~24px, then hold right+B for ~30 frames to build speed, then jump. Let me craft: [{left,20},{right,b,30},{right,b,a,20},{right,b,20},...]. Hmm but left for 20 frames moves him left ~20px, enough to build speed over 30 frames (speed max in ~20 frames).\n\nThen jump at 30 frames of running → he'd be past the wall already? Wait he's at the wall; backing 20px then running 30 frames at up to 2.5px/f covers ~60px, so he'd hit the wall at frame ~10 and jump wouldn't be pressed yet. Need to time the jump to press ~5 frames before hitting the wall.\n\nAlternative simpler: Back off then do a single big jump: left 20, then right+b for 12 frames (covers ~20px, reaching the wall), then right+b+a for 24 frames (jump). Speed at jump ~ building during the 12 frames — at 12 frames of running he'd have speed maybe 1.5px/f, decent.\n\nActually, from standing, SMB Mario accelerates to 24 (1.5px/f walk) in ~... and with B (run) to ~40 (2.5px/f). Over 30 frames he'd reach near max.\n\nLet me just back off more: left 30 frames (~30px back), then right+b for 20 frames (build speed, covers ~35px, ends at wall), then right+b+a 24 frames (jump). When he reaches the wall with speed and jumps, he should carry over.\n\nHmm, but if he reaches the wall before the jump, speed drops to 0.\n\nBetter: back off 40px, run right+B for 15 frames (accelerating, covers ~18px, still 22px from wall), then jump (right+b+a 24) — during jump he keeps moving right and gains height before reaching the wall. That should clear.\n\nLet me construct: [{left,32}, {right,b,16}, {right,b,a,26}, {right,b,20}] = 94 frames. Let me try that, then check position.\n\nActually, even simpler and very robust: since we're invincible and just need to REACH the Koopa for observation (not necessarily play legitimately), but the task says play to it. Using the checkpoint and save states is fine.\n\nHmm, but there's an even easier path: the task says the Koopa is at level x~1650 and \"an earlier session found it... it appears as the camera nears it\". I could instead just place Mario near it by writing $006D/$0086 — but stale camera issue: writing player position without the camera breaks. The play-to-reach skill explicitly says don't do that.\n\nSo keep playing. Let me load_state 1 (back to level start) and use a better script that jumps earlier before pipes. But the earlier pipes were cleared fine by the cycle (x 40→898 means he passed the first pipe/pit... actually did he? The first pipe in 1-1 is at x~640? He reached 898, so yes he passed obstacles). Actually wait, are there obstacles before x898? In 1-1: first Goomba, then ? blocks, then a pipe at x~640? Actually 1-1 layout: small pipe around x 640, tall pipe around x 900. He got to the tall pipe. So my cycle cleared the Goomba and first pipe.\n\nThe stuck is at the tall pipe. Let me back off and do a running jump.\n\nI'll do it from the current state (no need to reload): [{left,40},{right,b,14},{right,b,a,28},{right,b,30},{right,b,a,28},{right,b,30}] etc. Hmm risky.\n\nSimplest: [{left,40},{right,b,a,30},{right,b,30},{right,b,a,30},{right,b,30},{right,b,a,30},{right,b,30}] — with 40px backoff, then immediately jump+run. The first jump starts with little speed but gains height and drifts right; since he starts 40px away, by the time he reaches the pipe he should be near apex and moving. Might clear.\n\nActually a jump from standstill: SMB max jump is about 4 blocks (32px) high while drifting forward maybe 60px. The pipe ~50px tall. Running jump goes higher (~5 blocks). Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider the pipe height. The screenshot: pipe rim top at y≈148. Ground top at y≈200 (the brick surface). So pipe is 52px ≈ 6.5 tiles?? That can't be a standard pipe. Actually looking again: the pipe in the screenshot spans from y~150 to y~207, and the ground bricks start at y~200. Wait the ground surface is at y~200 (where Mario stands). The pipe's top rim at y~148 → 52px above ground = 6.5 tiles. Hmm, but SMB pipes are 2,3,4 tiles tall. The tall pipe in 1-1 is 4 tiles (32px? no, 4*16=64px). Hmm pipes are 16px per tile; pipe height 4 tiles = 64px? Actually the tall pipe in 1-1 is 4 tiles tall = 64 px, with rim 16px. Mario's jump can clear ~4 tiles = 64px? It's marginal; you need a running jump.\n\nAnyway. Maybe easier: he's stuck at the pipe; but note the Koopa is at x 1650, which is beyond this pipe. The known earlier session's checkpoint slot 4 was at x~1758 \"just past the Koopa Troopa\". If slot 4 exists from the prior session, I could load it and go back a little. But slots may be stale/different session. Actually the mapping says \"Session checkpoints: save slot 3 ... slot 4