ppearance. Actually the emergent item rises out; its sprite is drawn based on the object type. If we change the type while it's rising, it may switch to the star graphic. Worth trying.\n\nAlternatively, change $0039=2 BEFORE the item comes out: i.e., hit the block and immediately (same frame or next) write 2. But we can't easily time it.\n\nBetter: find where the item type is set. The block-hit routine sets it. If we can pause right after hitting the block (item still emerging), we can change the type and see.\n\nHmm, but there may be a separate \"object type\" byte for the spawned item, e.g. the powerup uses $0016 (enemy type array?) No — powerups aren't in the enemy arrays.\n\nLet me think about SMB1 internals (from memory of the disassembly):\n- When a block is hit, the routine at $? sets the powerup: there's a \"PowerUpType\" at $0039? Actually there's a variable \"Block_Item\"? Hmm.\n- In SMB1, the powerup object is drawn by the \"powerup\" code and its type is stored at $0039? I recall \"PowerUpType\" at $0039 is the type to spawn; and the active power-up's position/animation use $0016-based slots? Not sure.\n\nThe known mapping explicitly says: \"CPU $D80C LDA $39 (item type)\" — so $39 is the item type read at pickup. And the note \"item type 2 -> star timer\". So $39=2 means star.\n\nWhere does $39 get set? Likely when the item is spawned from the block. So if we write $39=2 right after hitting the block (before pickup), then when Mario picks it up, he'd get a star. But the SPRITE shown would still be a mushroom (drawn from a different variable? or from $39?).\n\nThe task: \"make one appear (for example by finding where the game keeps the emerging item's type and changing it before the item comes out)\" and then \"pause while it is on screen, and list_sprites on it\". So we need the starman SPRITE on screen. So $39 must control the sprite too.\n\nLet me just test empirically.\n\nPlan:\n- load_state 1, write $079F=0 (clear star to avoid palette flashing affe