nues over several frames). If so, setting $39 before the hit gets overwritten.\n\nWait, but the timing: $39 was 2 for frames 2-6 (after the load, during the jump start), then 0 at frame 8. So during the jump (before the hit at ~frame 10?), $39 was already zeroed. Hmm, the jump apex was frame ~10 (206: 168,160,153,147,141(apex),142...). Actually the hit happens when his head reaches the block, around frame 6-8 maybe ($00CE 153-147). So the block hit occurred around frame 6-8, and the routine overwrote $39.\n\nSo the routine DID overwrite $39 to 0 at the hit. That means the BCS branch was not taken → $39 was < 2 at the check. But we wrote 2... \n\nUnless the routine computes A from something else before the check. Let me re-read:\n$BC6B: A5 39   LDA $39\n$BC6D: C9 02   CMP #$02\n$BC6F: B0 0A   BCS +10 → $BC7B\nIf $39=2, C=1, BCS taken → skip to $BC7B, preserving $39=2.\n\nBut the watch shows $39 went 2→0. So either the branch wasn't taken (impossible if $39=2), or another write occurred.\n\nHmm, unless our write of $39=2 happened but then a different routine... \n\nOH WAIT. Maybe the routine at $BC60 is NOT the block-hit spawn; maybe it's the POWERUP SPAWN and it's called each frame during the \"block bump\" animation. And before it, something sets A. Hmm.\n\nActually, look again at the disassembly:\n$BC60: A9 01  LDA #$01\n$BC62: 85 23  STA $23\n$BC64: 85 14  STA $14\n$BC66: A9 03  LDA #$03\n$BC68: 8D 9F 04 STA $049F\n$BC6B: A5 39  LDA $39\n$BC6D: C9 02  CMP #$02\n$BC6F: B0 0A  BCS $BC7B\n$BC71: AD 56 07 LDA $0756\n$BC74: C9 02  CMP #$02\n$BC76: 90 01  BCC $BC7B\n$BC78: 4A     LSR A\n$BC79: 85 39  STA $39\n$BC7B: A9 20  LDA #$20\n...\n\nHmm wait, if $39 >= 2 → branch to $BC7B. If $39 < 2 → LDA $0756; if $0756 < 2 → branch to $BC7B (preserving $39); else LSR A (A=$0756=2 → 1) and STA $39=1.\n\nSo for small Mario ($0756=0), $39 is preserved (branch at $BC76 since 0 < 2). So writing $39=2 before the hit should be preserved!\n\nBut the watch shows it became 0. So the writer must be elsewhere, OR the routine wasn't the cause.\n\nHmm, unless $39 was zeroed by the \"powerup pickup\" check or by some other code not found by the search (e.g., indirect). Let me search for \"86 39\" (STX $39) and \"84 39\" (STY $39), and also \"A9 00 85 39\"? The latter includes 85 39 so search \"85 39\" would find it (it found 0x3C79 = BC79 only).\n\nHmm. So only one writer. Then how did $39 become 0?\n\nUnless the search \"85 39\" missed because the operand bytes differ? No.\n\nWait — maybe the emulator's memory read watch showed $39's value but my write of $39=2 was to address 57 = 0x39. Yes.\n\nHmm, and the watch values: first sample frame 2 = 2, frames 2,4,6 = 2, then frame 8 = 0. So something wrote 0 between frames 6 and 8.\n\nMaybe the routine at $BC60 ran with $39 = 2 → BCS to $BC7B → preserved. So not it.\n\nCould the game have run a different store like \"STA $39,X\" with X=0? e.g., \"95 39\"? Let me search \"95 39\".\n\nActually, maybe there's an STA $0039 via \"8D 39 00\" (absolute). Search for \"8D 39 00\".\n\nLet me also consider that $39 might be part of a block cleared by a loop (e.g., clearing zero page $39 during some init). Hmm.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually maybe the routine at $BC60 is the \"powerup spawn\" and it's called with A already loaded? No, it loads.\n\nHmm, let me just search for other stores.\n\nActually, I realize: maybe $39 was zeroed NOT by a store but... no, memory only changes by writes.\n\nLet me search \"8D 39 00\" and \"95 39\".\n\nLet me also reconsider: maybe my write didn't have time; the watch sample at frame 2 = 2 (after play_input resumed). Good.\n\nBetween frame 6 and 8, the block hit occurred (head at 153→147). The routine may have run at the hit. If $39=2, BCS preserves. So it should stay 2.\n\nUnless... the LDA $39 / CMP #$02 at $BC6B is for a DIFFERENT purpose and the routine writes $39 elsewhere. Hmm, no.\n\nWait — maybe the jump hit the block at frame ~8, and the routine at $BC60 (powerup spawn) ran, but there