ess 27) = 0x2F, step, list_sprites; then 0x30, etc.\n\nBut the item's sprite might be determined by $39 at spawn, not the type. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively — the real trick from the task: \"finding where the game keeps the emerging item's type and changing it before the item comes out\". So there IS a byte that, if changed before the item comes out, changes the item. We changed $39 but the sprite didn't change — maybe because we changed it too late (after the item was already spawned with its type determined).\n\nActually, wait: in the test, we hit the block (mushroom spawned), THEN wrote $39=2, stepped. The mushroom sprite persisted. If the sprite were chosen from $39 at spawn, too late.\n\nSo the right approach: set the byte BEFORE the item comes out — i.e., before the block is hit. We tried setting $39=2 before the hit, but it got zeroed during the jump.\n\nHmm, but the zeroing: maybe it happened because the block-hit routine ran on a DIFFERENT block? During the jump at level 335, Mario jumps straight up and hits the block at 336 — the spawn routine runs and (with $39=2) should skip. But $39 became 0.\n\nUnless the routine read $39 BEFORE our write took effect? No, our write was before.\n\nHmm, unless write_memory to CPU RAM while paused gets overwritten by the pause's \"one frame step\"? load_state runs one frame; then write_memory writes 2. Then play_input resumes. Watch frame 2 = 2. Good.\n\nThen at frame ~7, the routine ran. If it read 2 → skip. But result 0.\n\nWait, maybe there's ANOTHER call to the routine BEFORE the hit: the \"block bump\" of the ? block when hit... no.\n\nHmm, actually! Maybe the routine at $BC60 runs EVERY FRAME as part of the powerup spawn state machine, and on the first frame it runs (when Mario is near the block?), $39 was 2 → skip; but it also sets $23=1,$14=1,... hmm.\n\nActually wait, maybe I have the wrong idea about which routine is the spawn. $BC60 sets $23=1, $14=1, $049f=3 — those look like \"bump block\" animation variables, and $