rman!\n\nLet me read them as a composite: $8D at (186,130) and (194,130) — two side by side; $E4 at (186,138) and (194,138). With flip_h on the right ones. So the star is 16x16, made of:\n- upper-left: $8D\n- upper-right: $8D flip_h\n- lower-left: $E4\n- lower-right: $E4 flip_h\n\n$8D is the top-left of the star (a point going up-right) and $E4 the bottom (star points). Combined they form a star shape. Plausible!\n\nBut the palette: reported 7. Hmm. The real starman in SMB1 is yellow-ish. Palette 7 in 1-1 = [$0F,$0F,$36,$17] (black, black, peach, brown) — that would render the star with peach/brown colors. Hmm, that doesn't look like a yellow star. \n\nBut wait — maybe the game switches sprite palette 7 for the star? In SMB1, sprite palette 7 is [$16 red, $27 orange, $30 white...]? Hmm, the Goomba mapping said palette 7 live = [$0F,$0F,$36,$17]. Hmm.\n\nHmm, actually in SMB1 the star item might be drawn with palette 7 which in most levels is the \"star\" palette (yellow/white). But in 1-1 the same palette is used by the Goomba (brown). Hmm, that would make the star brown.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe the attr base for X=2 is 1 (not 2), giving palette 5? Hmm.\n\nActually, let me not over-think the palette: the task asks for the notes to give \"each OAM tile id with its x/y offset, flips and sprite palette\". The list_sprites reports palette 7 (attr $23/$63). Hmm, but this may be affected by our $03CA leftover.\n\nHmm, but actually — for a REAL star (spawned naturally), what palette would it use? Let me check by computing: attr = $E6CE[X] | $03CA. $E6CE[2] = 02. $03CA is set by the spawn routine at $BC7B to $20 (as we disassembled: LDA #$20; STA $03CA). Wait, the disassembly showed:\n$BC7B: A9 20 LDA #$20\n$BC7D: 8D CA 03 STA $03CA\nAh wait, that was in the earlier disassembly: \"$bc7b: a9 20 lda #0x20 / $bc7d: 8d ca 03 sta 0x03ca\". So $03CA = $20.\n\nThen attr = 02 | 20 = 22 → palette 6. But list_sprites says 35 ($23). Hmm, 0x23 = 0x02|0x21. So maybe there's also bit0 set (flip_h) → attr $62/$63? Ah: the OAM attr also includes flip_h ($40) and priority. list_sprites reports attr 35 = 0x23 and 99 = 0x63 for the flip_h one. So base attr for non-flipped = 0x23 → low bits 3 → palette 7. Hmm.\n\nHmm, 0x23 = 0b00100011. Bits: 5=0x20 (priority? actually bit5 = sprite priority (behind bg) = 0x20), bit0-1 = palette. Low bits = 3 → palette 7.\n\nHmm, so the base table value for X=2 must be 03, not 02. Let me recheck the table bytes: indices 78-81 = [2,1,2,1]. Hmm, so [2,1,2,1]. X=2 → 2. Hmm.\n\nUnless the table is at $E6CE = 0x66CE = 26318; 26318-26240 = 78. bytes[78]=2. So $E6CE[0]=2, [1]=1, [2]=2, [3]=1. Hmm.\n\nThen attr = 2 | 0x03CA. If $03CA = 0x21, attr = 0x23. read_memory gave $03CA = 32 = 0x20. Hmm. Then attr should be 0x22.\n\nBut list shows 0x23. Hmm. Unless the OAM attr was written by the later code at $E71A: `ORA $03CA` after AND #3 of $09... Let me re-read:\n$E712: LDA $09\n$E714: LSR A\n$E715: AND #03\n$E717: ORA $03CA\n$E71A: STA $0202,y\nThat's for the \"flash\" perhaps. Hmm.\n\nThis is a rabbit hole. The key deliverable: the starman metasprite = tiles $8D,$8D,$E4,$E4 with flip_h on the second of each pair, palette 6 or 7.\n\nHmm, but I want the REAL starman, not a hacked-palette one. Actually, the sprite is the real starman tiles; the palette is whatever the game computes (7).\n\nHmm, but wait: is this really the starman? Let me verify the shape by looking at the screenshot. The screenshot shows a small dark shape on the block — not clearly a star.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider: maybe X=2 is not the star. The $D80C code: LDA $39 / CMP #2 / BCC powerup / CMP #3 / BEQ (1-up?) / else star. So $39>=2 and !=3 → star. So $39=2 → star. Good. And the sprite table X=2 → tiles $8D,$E4. So the starman's tiles are $8D/$E4. \n\nBut wait, in SMB1 the starman item is actually the \"star\" with a face. Tiles $8D/$E4... let me visualize: $8D is mostly index-3 pixels forming a diagonal wedge; $E4 forms another wedge. Combined and mirrored, they'd fo